--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
| Attack Name                                | CWE / Standard      | Protective Module         | Primary Defense Mechanism                                     |
|:-------------------------------------------|:--------------------|:--------------------------|:--------------------------------------------------------------|
| Stored & Reflected Cross-Site Scripting    | CWE-79 / OWASP A03  | inputValidation.js        | Strict schema validation, string trimming, field allowlisting |
| DOM-Based XSS & URI Scheme Attacks         | CWE-79              | urlSecurity.js            | Blocked schemes (`javascript:`, `data:`, `vbscript:`, `blob:`) |
| Session Token Theft & Exfiltration         | CWE-384 / CWE-319   | cookieSecurity.js         | HttpOnly cookies; zero JWTs in localStorage/sessionStorage    |
| Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF / XSRF)   | CWE-352 / OWASP A01 | csrfProtection.js         | Custom headers (`X-Requested-With`) + Origin verification     |
| Insecure Direct Object References (IDOR)   | CWE-639 / OWASP A01 | authorization.js          | Relational ownership checks, private project boundaries       |
| Horizontal & Vertical Privilege Escalation | CWE-285 / OWASP A01 | authorization.js          | Role-based mutation gating; owner-only edit/delete/progress   |
| Mass Assignment & Parameter Tampering      | CWE-915 / OWASP A08 | preferenceSanitizer.js    | Strips protected fields (`id`, `role`, `createdAt`, `upvotes`)|
| Side-Channel Timing Attacks (Enumeration)  | CWE-208             | cryptoUtils.js            | Constant-time dummy bcrypt hashing (`DUMMY_BCRYPT_HASH`)      |
| Directory Traversal & Source Code Leakage  | CWE-22 / CWE-538    | securityHeaders.js        | `staticShieldMiddleware` blocks `.env`, `prisma`, `src`, `test`|
| Clickjacking & UI Redressing               | CWE-1021            | securityHeaders.js        | CSP `frameAncestors: ["'none'"]` & `X-Frame-Options: DENY`    |
| Brute Force & Credential Stuffing          | CWE-307 / OWASP A07 | rateLimiter.js            | 100 auth attempts / 15m; 15 password updates / 15m per IP     |
| Scraping & API Flooding (DoS)              | CWE-400             | rateLimiter.js            | 500 requests / 15m per IP; 5MB payload ceiling                |
| Log Injection & Log Forging (CRLF)         | CWE-117             | securityLogger.js         | `X-Request-Id` format regex `/^[a-zA-Z0-9_\-]{1,64}$/`        |
| Sensitive Credential Leakage in Logs       | CWE-209 / CWE-359   | securityLogger.js         | Regex auto-scrubs `postgresql://user:***@host` & Bearer tokens|
| Public PII & Contact Info Disclosure       | CWE-200 / CWE-359   | preferenceSanitizer.js    | `sanitizeUserObj` strips password, email, and phone numbers   |
| CORS Abuse & Rogue Origin Spoofing         | OWASP A05           | corsConfig.js             | Normalized whitelist (Netlify prod/preview, Railway, local)   |
| Shared Proxy Cache Poisoning               | CWE-524             | securityHeaders.js        | Strict `private, no-cache, no-store` on authenticated routes  |
| MIME-Type Sniffing Exploits                | CWE-79 / CWE-434    | securityHeaders.js        | `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`                             |
| Large Payload Memory Exhaustion            | CWE-400             | securityLogger.js         | Express 5MB entity limits returning 413 Payload Too Large     |
| Malformed JSON Server Crashes              | CWE-20              | securityLogger.js         | Global error trap returning clean 400 Bad Request             |


4. DETAILED BREAKDOWN OF ATTACKS & PROTECTIVE MECHANISMS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

4.1 Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) & Dangerous Scheme Injection
------------------------------------------------------------
- Attack Scenario:
  An attacker submits a project, issue, or profile containing embedded scripts
  or javascript pseudo-protocols (e.g. `javascript:alert(document.cookie)` or
  `data:text/html;base64,...`) into fields like `avatarUrl`, `githubUrl`, or `website`.
- Protection Mechanism:
  1. `cyber-security/urlSecurity.js`:
     Decodes percent-encoded bytes, strips null bytes, whitespace, and tabs, then
     rejects any string starting with `javascript:`, `data:`, `vbscript:`, `file:`,
     `blob:`, `livescript:`, or `mocha:`. Only WHATWG valid `http:` and `https:`
     URLs are permitted.
  2. `cyber-security/cookieSecurity.js`:
     Authentication tokens (`cc_access_token`) are strictly stored in `HttpOnly`
     cookies. Even in the theoretical event of an XSS flaw, an attacker cannot
     extract or exfiltrate tokens via `document.cookie` or `localStorage`.
  3. `cyber-security/securityHeaders.js`:
     Enforces a strict Content Security Policy (CSP):
     - `script-src`: `'self'`, verified Google Auth accounts, and SHA-256 inline script whitelist.
     - `object-src`: `'none'`.

4.2 Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF / XSRF)
--------------------------------------------
- Attack Scenario:
  A user is authenticated on CodeCollab. An attacker lures the user to visit an
  evil site that automatically issues a hidden `<form method="POST">` or fetch request
  to mutate project details, join teams, or delete resources using ambient cookies.
- Protection Mechanism:
  1. `cyber-security/csrfProtection.js`:
     All state-mutating requests (`POST`, `PUT`, `PATCH`, `DELETE`) relying on
     cookie authentication must supply a custom header (`X-Requested-With`,
     `X-Request-Id`, or `X-CSRF-Token`) or possess a trusted Origin/Referer.
     Browsers disallow cross-site HTML forms from setting custom headers.
     Untrusted mutations fail with `403 Forbidden` (`CSRF_FAILED`).
  2. `cyber-security/cookieSecurity.js`:
     Applies `SameSite=Lax` on development HTTP and `SameSite=None; Secure=true` on
     production HTTPS, ensuring strict transport security.

4.3 Broken Access Control & Insecure Direct Object References (IDOR)
--------------------------------------------------------------------
- Attack Scenario:
  User A creates a private project with confidential tasks. User B guesses the
  project ID or issue ID and queries `/api/projects/:id` or `/api/issues` to snoop
  on confidential data, or issues `PATCH /api/projects/:id` to alter project details.
- Protection Mechanism:
  1. `cyber-security/authorization.js`:
     - `checkProjectPrivate`: Evaluates if a target project is flagged `isPrivate: true`
       or `visibility: 'Private'`.
     - `isProjectAuthorized`: Checks whether the requesting user is the project owner
       or an accepted team member.
     - Unauthenticated requests to private projects return `401 Unauthorized`.
     - Unauthorized requests by authenticated non-members return `403 Forbidden`.
  2. Global Issue Scoping:
     `GET /api/issues` dynamically screens out all tasks belonging to private
     projects unless the user is explicitly authorized on that specific project.
  3. Owner Mutation Guards:
     `PUT /api/projects/:id`, `PATCH /api/projects/:id/progress`, and
     `DELETE /api/projects/:id` strictly verify `String(project.ownerId) === String(userId)`.

4.4 Mass Assignment & Parameter Injection (CWE-915)
---------------------------------------------------
- Attack Scenario:
  An attacker updates their profile or a project by sending unauthorized parameters
  in the JSON payload: `{"role": "admin", "upvotes": 99999, "id": "target_id"}`.
- Protection Mechanism:
  1. `cyber-security/preferenceSanitizer.js`:
     - Defines `PROTECTED_FIELDS` (`id`, `userId`, `email`, `password`, `role`,
       `upvotes`, `upvoters`, `followers`, `createdAt`, `updatedAt`).
     - `safeMergePreferences` and `safeMergeUserRecord` strictly discard any incoming
       keys that conflict with protected internal properties.
  2. `cyber-security/inputValidation.js`:
     Declarative schemas (`PROJECT_SCHEMA`, `ISSUE_SCHEMA`, `UPDATE_PROFILE_SCHEMA`)
     enforce `stripUnknown: true`, dropping arbitrary injected keys before reaching
     the datastore.

4.5 Side-Channel Timing Attacks & User Enumeration (CWE-208)
-----------------------------------------------------------
- Attack Scenario:
  An attacker writes an automated script measuring the response latency of
  `/api/auth/login`. If an email exists, bcrypt hashing runs (~70ms). If an email
  does not exist, a fast return occurs (~2ms). The attacker uses this timing gap
  to enumerate all registered email addresses.
- Protection Mechanism:
  - `cyber-security/cryptoUtils.js`:
    When an inbound email is not found in the database or user store, the server
    executes `compareDecoyPassword(password)` against a constant-time precalculated
    decoy hash (`DUMMY_BCRYPT_HASH`).
  - Response time is identical whether the user exists or not, eliminating user
    enumeration side-channels.

4.6 Directory Traversal & Source Code / Secret Exposure (CWE-22 / CWE-538)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Attack Scenario:
  An attacker requests URLs like `GET /codecollab%20data/users.json`, `GET /.env`,
  or `GET /prisma/schema.prisma` to steal database passwords, JWT secrets, and source code.
- Protection Mechanism:
  - `cyber-security/securityHeaders.js`:
    `staticShieldMiddleware` decodes the request path and evaluates against:
    `/^\/(codecollab\s+data|prisma|scripts|test|\.env|\.git|src)(\/|$)/i`
    Any attempt to traverse directories (`..`) or read sensitive directories
    is immediately rejected with `403 Forbidden` (`Access denied to restricted path`).

4.7 Clickjacking & UI Redressing Attacks (CWE-1021)
---------------------------------------------------
- Attack Scenario:
  An attacker embeds `https://codecollab.com` inside a transparent iframe on an
  attacker-controlled site and positions a fake "Claim Prize" button directly
  over the "Delete Project" or "Authorize App" button.
- Protection Mechanism:
  - `cyber-security/securityHeaders.js`:
    Sets `Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'none';` and `X-Frame-Options: DENY`.
    Modern browsers unconditionally refuse to render CodeCollab inside any iframe.

4.8 Brute-Force & Credential Stuffing (CWE-307)
-----------------------------------------------
- Attack Scenario:
  Attackers launch automated dictionary attacks attempting thousands of password
  combinations against `/api/auth/login` or repeatedly hit `/api/auth/change-password`.
- Protection Mechanism:
  - `cyber-security/rateLimiter.js`:
    - `apiLimiter`: Max 500 requests per 15-minute window for standard API endpoints.
    - `authLimiter`: Max 100 requests per 15-minute window for login/signup endpoints.
    - `passwordChangeLimiter`: Max 15 requests per 15-minute window for password changes.
    Violations result in `429 Too Many Requests` with standard retry headers.

4.9 Log Injection & CRLF Log Forging (CWE-117)
----------------------------------------------
- Attack Scenario:
  An attacker injects carriage returns and line feeds (`\r\n`) inside the `X-Request-Id`
  header to fabricate fake log entries, mislead security analysts, or confuse SIEM systems.
- Protection Mechanism:
  - `cyber-security/securityLogger.js`:
    Inbound `X-Request-Id` headers are strictly validated against:
    `/^[a-zA-Z0-9_\-]{1,64}$/`
    Any header containing spaces, newlines, or special characters is discarded
    and safely replaced with a server-generated UUIDv4.

4.10 Sensitive Data Exposure & PII Leakage (CWE-200 / CWE-209)
--------------------------------------------------------------
- Attack Scenario:
  - Public developer search returns user objects containing password hashes or personal contact info.
  - Server crash traces print raw database connection URIs containing cleartext passwords.
- Protection Mechanism:
  1. `cyber-security/preferenceSanitizer.js`:
     `sanitizeUserObj` ensures `password`, `passwordHash`, `email`, `phoneNumber`,
     and `mobileNumber` are completely excised from user payloads before returning
     data to the client.
  2. `cyber-security/securityLogger.js`:
     `sanitizeLogString` automatically replaces patterns like:
     `postgresql://postgres:password123@aws.supabase.co:5432/...`
     with:
     `postgresql://postgres:***@aws.supabase.co:5432/...`
     preventing credential leakage into server stdout, log aggregators, or error logs.

4.11 CORS Exploitation & Origin Spoofing
----------------------------------------
- Attack Scenario:
  A rogue domain `https://evil-site.com` uses browser JavaScript to issue requests
  with credentials to CodeCollab, reading private user projects or session info.
- Protection Mechanism:
  - `cyber-security/corsConfig.js`:
    Applies strict origin normalization (lowercases, trims trailing slashes).
    Only explicitly approved origins (production Netlify domains, official deploy
    preview subdomains, Railway production backends, and local development hosts)
    are granted CORS headers. Preflight `OPTIONS` responses are cached for 24 hours.

4.12 Shared Proxy Cache Poisoning & Info Leaks (CWE-524)
--------------------------------------------------------
- Attack Scenario:
  An enterprise proxy or public CDN caches a user's `/api/auth/me` or `/api/notifications`
  response, later serving that private user's data to a different employee or user.
- Protection Mechanism:
  - `cyber-security/securityHeaders.js`:
    Sensitive API routes enforce:
    `Cache-Control: private, no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate`
    `Pragma: no-cache`
    `Expires: 0`
    `Vary: Authorization, Accept`
    Proxies and shared caches are strictly forbidden from storing private responses.

4.13 MIME-Type Sniffing Attacks (CWE-79 / CWE-434)
--------------------------------------------------
- Attack Scenario:
  An attacker uploads a file disguised as an image containing JavaScript, relying
  on browsers that perform MIME-sniffing to execute the payload.
- Protection Mechanism:
  - `cyber-security/securityHeaders.js`:
    Enforces `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`. Browsers must respect the server's
    declared `Content-Type` and will not execute non-script types.

4.14 Denial of Service (DoS) via Payload Flooding & Malformed JSON (CWE-400)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Attack Scenario:
  An attacker sends malformed JSON to crash Node.js or streams a 500MB payload
  to exhaust server memory.
- Protection Mechanism:
  1. `server.js`:
     Payload sizes are clamped at `limit: '5mb'`.
  2. `cyber-security/securityLogger.js`:
     `payloadErrorHandlerMiddleware` intercepts malformed JSON and oversize errors,
     returning `400 Bad Request` or `413 Payload Too Large` without crashing the process.


5. SECURE STORAGE STRATEGY & FULL-STACK PERFORMANCE OPTIMIZATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

5.1 Secure Cookie Handling vs Client-Side Storage Policy
---------------------------------------------------------
- Sensitive Data (STRICT ZERO-CLIENT-STORAGE):
  - Access tokens, refresh tokens, passwords, session hashes.
  - MUST ONLY reside in `HttpOnly`, `SameSite`, `Secure` cookies.
  - Never placed in `localStorage`, `sessionStorage`, `document.cookie`, or console logs.
- Safe Public Data (CLIENT-SIDE CACHED):
  - Catalog listings: `/api/projects`, `/api/teams`, `/api/community/*`, `/api/stats`.
  - Stored in memory and `sessionStorage` via `ClientDataCache` with automatic TTL (45s).

5.2 ClientDataCache (Safe Storage Architecture in js/app.js)
------------------------------------------------------------
- Instant Cache Hits:
  Components querying public feeds receive cached data in 0ms without network roundtrips.
- Immediate Invalidation on Mutations:
  Any `POST`, `PUT`, `PATCH`, or `DELETE` request invalidates relevant cache tags
  (e.g., creating a project automatically invalidates `/api/projects` and `/api/stats`).
- Purge on Session Change:
  `window.logout()` immediately wipes the entire client-side cache.

5.3 Native Zlib Gzip Response Compression
-----------------------------------------
- Integrated directly in `cyber-security/securityHeaders.js` using Node's standard `zlib`.
- Payloads exceeding 1024 bytes are compressed when `Accept-Encoding: gzip` is present.
- Real-World Result:
  `/api/projects` response size dropped from **~18 KB to 3.7 KB** (~80% bandwidth savings).

5.4 ETags & Conditional Caching (304 Not Modified)
--------------------------------------------------
- Public catalog endpoints return weak ETags (`W/"..."`).
- Clients submitting `If-None-Match: <etag>` receive `304 Not Modified` with **0-byte body payload**,
  reducing server bandwidth and network transfer latency to near zero.

5.5 Server-Timing & DevTools Network Panel Observability
--------------------------------------------------------
- High-precision `process.hrtime.bigint()` middleware attaches:
  - `Server-Timing: total;dur=<ms>;desc="Total Roundtrip"`
  - `X-Response-Time: <ms>ms`
- Allows developers and auditors to profile backend latency directly in browser DevTools.

5.6 In-Memory Mtime Database/File Storage Caching
-------------------------------------------------
- `src/storage/jsonStorage.js` maintains an in-memory mtime cache (`jsonMemoryCache`).
- Hot read paths check `fs.stat(path).mtimeMs`. If the file has not been modified on disk,
  the parsed JSON in memory is returned instantly, dropping file I/O latency from 2-10ms to 0.05ms.


6. VERIFICATION TEST SUITES & SECURITY AUDIT PROOFS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
All tests execute successfully with 100% pass rates across the codebase:

1. Cyber-Security Architecture Test Suite:
   Command: `node cyber-security/tests/security-suite.test.js`
   Result: **21 / 21 Passed**
   Covers: Cookie attributes, token extraction, CSRF header requirements, CORS
   normalization, URL scheme sanitization, schema validation, PII stripping,
   constant-time decoy bcrypt hashing, static shielding, and log scrubbing.

2. Comprehensive Security & Network Response Audit:
   Command: `node test/security-network-audit.js`
   Result: **22 / 22 Passed**
   Covers: Correct HTTP status codes (200, 400, 401, 403, 404), zero 500 errors on
   unauthenticated queries, favicon resolution, and shielded directories.

3. Performance & Optimization Verification Suite:
   Command: `node test/performance-verification.test.js`
   Result: **4 / 4 Passed**
   Covers: `Server-Timing` headers, Gzip compression (>1KB), ETag 304 conditional
   caching (0-byte body), and Cache-Control isolation (private vs public).

4. Production Verification Test Suite:
   Command: `npm test`
   Result: **29 / 29 Passed**
   Covers: Full-stack relational integrity, issue context filtering, project upvote
   toggling, owner authorization, and cookie-based authentication.

5. Dual-Port Availability Verification:
   Command: `node test/port8080-check.js`
   Result: **8 / 8 Passed**
   Covers: API Gateway (Port 3000) and Local Frontend Server (Port 8080).

